<section xml:id="sec_circ-practice" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Practice - Electric Circuits</title>
	
	<!--
	If you want an A*R*C*S (Analyze-Represent-Calculate-Sensemake) activity, use the <project> tag.
	If you want an Explain activity, use the <investigation> tag.
		Use this text for qualitative comparisons: <em>greater than</em>, <em>less than</em>, or <em>equal to</em>
	If you want a Calculate activity, use the <exploration> tag.
	
	<m># \mathrm{~}</m>
	<m> \mathrm{~}</m>
	-->

	<subsubsection>
		<title>Explanation Practice</title>
		
		<investigation>
			<title>Circuit I</title>
			<statement><p></p>
				<figure xml:id="fig-circuit_1">
					<caption>Circuit diagram. The top and bottom circuits are identical, apart from an extra wire connecting around lightbulb C in the bottom diagram.</caption>
					<image source="circ/circuit-1.png" width = "40%" >
					<description>Top: A circuit diagram with a battery on the left, bulbs A and D on the top, bulbs B and C on a branch extending downward from between A and D, and an extra wire extending downward past bulb D.  Below: An otherwise identical circuit diagram with an extra wire connecting the two ends of bulb C directly.</description></image>
				</figure>
				<p>
					The circuit shown on top in the image above has four identical light bulbs and an ideal battery.
				</p>
				<ol>
					<li>
						<p>
							Rank the bulbs in the top circuit according to brightness, from brightest to dimmest.
						</p>
						<p>
							A wire is now added to the circuit, as shown in the bottom diagram.
						</p>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>
							Does the brightness of bulb C increase, decrease, or remain the same?
						</p>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>
							Does the brightness of bulb A increase, decrease, or remain the same?
						</p>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>
							Does the current through the battery increase, decrease, or remain the same?
						</p>
					</li>
				</ol>
			</statement>
		</investigation>

			<investigation>
		<title>Circuit II</title>
		<statement><p></p>
			<figure xml:id="fig-circuit_2">
				<caption>Circuit diagram.</caption>
				<image source="circ/circuit-2.png" width = "40%" >
				<description>A circuit diagram with a battery on the left.  On the right, a branch with bulb A in series with a parallel network of bulbs B and C, in series with bulb D.</description></image>
			</figure>
			<p>
				The circuit above shows four identical bulbs connected to an ideal battery. The switch is initially closed.
			</p>
			<ol>
				<li>
					<p>
						Rank the bulbs according to brightness, in order from brightest to dimmest. If two bulbs have the same brightness, indicate that explicitly.
					</p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<p>
						When the switch is opened, will bulb A get brighter, get dimmer, or remain the same brightness?
					</p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<p>
						When the switch is opened, will bulb B get brighter, get dimmer, or remain the same brightness?
					</p>
				</li>
			</ol>
		</statement>
	</investigation>

	<investigation>
		<title>Circuit III</title>
		<statement><p></p>
			<figure xml:id="fig-circuit_8">
				<caption>Circuit diagram.</caption>
				<image source="circ/circuit-8.png" width = "40%" >
				<description>A circuit with a battery in series with a parallel network consisting of resistor A and a series network of D and B and C in parallel, all in series with E.</description></image>
			</figure>
			<p>
				The circuit above includes five identical linear resistors with resistance 5 <m>\Omega</m>. The battery provides 32 V.
			</p>
			<p>
				Identify the resistor(s) with the lowest current and determine this amount of current. Show your work and describe your problem-solving process.
			</p>
		
		<assemblage>
			<p>
				<alert><!--REPLACE_TAG-->Tip:</alert> Create a voltge diagram for each branch of this circuit.
			</p>
		</assemblage>
	</statement>
	</investigation>

	<investigation>
			<title>Circuit IV</title>
			<statement><p></p>
				<figure xml:id="fig-circuit_4">
					<caption>Circuit diagram.</caption>
					<image source="circ/circuit-4.png" width = "40%" >
					<description>A circuit diagram with three vertical branches, from left to right: a battery, bulb 11, and bulbs 12 and another battery in series.</description></image>
				</figure>
				<p>
					Recall the circuit from class, reproduced above. Suppose batteries A and B are not identical. 
				</p>
				<ol>
					<li>
						<p>
							Suppose that the absolute value of the potential difference across battery B were reduced, but battery A is unchanged.
						</p>
						<ol>
							<li>
								<p>
									When the switch is closed, would bulb 12 light?
								</p>
							</li>
							<li>
								<p>
									When the switch is closed, would bulb 11 be brighter than, dimmer than, or as bright as it was in class?
								</p>
							</li>
						</ol>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>
							Suppose instead that the absolute value of the potential difference across battery A were reduced, but battery B were unchanged (the same as it was in class). 
						</p>
						<ol>
							<li>
								<p>
									When the switch is closed, would bulb 12 light?
								</p>
							</li>
							<li>
								<p>
									When the switch is closed, would bulb 11 be brighter than, dimmer than, or as bright as it was in class?
								</p>
							</li>
						</ol>
					</li>
				</ol>
			</statement>
		</investigation>

		<investigation>
			<title>Circuit V</title>
			<statement><p></p>
				<figure xml:id="fig-circuit_5">
					<caption>Circuit diagram.</caption>
					<image source="bb-circuit5.png" width = "40%" >
					<description>A circuit diagram with three vertical branches, from left to right: battery A, bulb C, and bulb D and battery B in series.  Point 1 lies above bulb 1, point 2 between bulb 2 and battery D, and point 3 below bulb 1.</description></image>
				</figure>
				<p>
					Consider the circuit shown above, which is a variant of the circuit from class. All bulbs are identical, and all batteries are ideal and identical. (Note that the orientation of battery B has changed.)
				</p>
				<ol>
					<li>
						<p>
							Draw arrows to indicate the current through each circuit element.
						</p>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>
							Sketch appropriate voltage diagrams for this circuit.
						</p>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>
							Compare the brightness of the light bulbs.
						</p>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>
							Use your diagrams to rank the voltages across all bulbs and batteries.
						</p>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>
							Rank the currents through all circuit elements.
						</p>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>
							Suppose that bulb C were removed, leaving a gap in the circuit. Would this cause bulb D to change brightness?
						</p>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>
							Suppose that battery A were removed, leaving a gap in the circuit. Would this cause either bulb to change brightness?
						</p>
					</li>
				</ol>
			</statement>
		</investigation>

		
	</subsubsection>

	<subsubsection>
		<title>A*R*C*S Practice</title>
		
		<project>
			<title>Current</title>
			<statement><p></p>
				<p>
					The total amount of charge that has entered a wire at time <m>t</m> is given by: <me>
						Q(t) = Q_o (1 - e^{t/\tau})
					</me>
					Find an expression for the current in the wire at time <m>t</m>.
				</p>
			</statement>
		</project>


	</subsubsection>
		
	<subsubsection>
	<title>Numerical Practice</title>

		<exploration>
			<title>Lightning Bolt</title>
			<statement><p></p>
				<p>
					A large lightning bolt had a <m>20,000 \mathrm{~A}</m> current and moved <m>30.0 \mathrm{~C}</m> of charge.  What was its duration? 
				</p>
			</statement>
			<answer>
				<p>
					<m>1.50 \mathrm{~ms}</m>
				</p>
			</answer>
		</exploration>

		<exploration>
			<title>How many electrons?</title>
			<statement><p></p>
				<p>
					How many electrons flow through a point in a wire in <m>3.00 \mathrm{~s}</m> if there is a constant current of <m>I = 4.00 \mathrm{~A}</m>?
				</p>
			</statement>
			<answer>
				<p>
					<m>7.5 \times 10^{19}</m> electrons
				</p>
			</answer>
		</exploration>
		
		<exploration>
			<title>Current</title>
			<statement><p></p>
				<p>
					The quantity of charge through a conductor is modeled as <m>Q(t) = (4.0 \frac{\mathrm{C}}{\mathrm{s}^4}t^4 - (1.0 \frac{\mathrm{C}}{\mathrm{s}})t + 6.0 \mathrm{mC}</m>. What is the current at time t = 3.0 s?
				</p>
			</statement>
			<answer>
				<p>
					<m>0.431 \mathrm{~A}</m>
				</p>
			</answer>
		</exploration>

		<figure xml:id="os-junction">
			<title>Junction</title>
			<caption>Junction with three incoming current branches.</caption>
			<image source="circ/junction.jpg" width = "50%" >
			<description>Current I2 moving to the right, I1 moving down, and I3 moving to the left.</description></image>
		</figure>
		<exploration>
			<title>Junction</title>
			<statement><p></p>
				<p>
					Can all of the currents going into the junction shown above be positive? Explain.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<answer>
				<p>
					No. At least one current must leave the junction, and at least one must enter the junction.
				</p>
			</answer>
		</exploration>

		<figure xml:id="bs-current-voltage">
			<title>Current versus Voltage</title>
			<caption>Current versus voltage graph.</caption>
			<image source="circ/current-vs-voltage.png" width = "50%" >
			<description>A graph of current vs voltage that starts at the origin and increases linearly.</description></image>
		</figure>

		<exploration>
			<title>Resistance from a Graph</title>
			<statement><p></p>
				<p>
					Suppose you apply several different voltage differences across a circuit and measure the resulting current through the circuit; a plot of your results is shown above.  What is the resistance of the circuit?
				</p>
			</statement>
			<answer>
				<p>
					<m>1000 \mathrm{~\Omega}</m>
				</p>
			</answer>
		</exploration>

		<exploration>
			<title>Bird on a Wire</title>
			<statement><p></p>
				<p>
					High-voltage overhead electric lines are not covered by insulation, unlike wires that are used in electric circuitry in homes. Explain why a bird that lands on a single high-voltage line does not get electrocuted.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<answer>
				<p>
					The bird's feet, touching the high-voltage line, do form a complete circuit through the bird, but essentially no current flows through the bird, because the resistance value of the wire is essentially zero between the points where the bird's feet are touching it; whereas the bird has some non-negligible resistance.
				</p>
			</answer>
		</exploration>
	
		<figure xml:id="bs-circuit-11">
			<title>Circuit Diagram</title>
			<caption>A circuit diagram.</caption>
			<image source="circ/circuit-11.png" width = "50%" >
			<description>A circuit diagram with a battery, R1, and R2 in series.</description></image>
		</figure>

		<exploration>
			<title>Circuit Quantities</title>
			<statement><p></p>
				<p>
					Consider the circuit above. The voltage drops across resistors 1 and 2 are <m>3 \mathrm{~V}</m> and <m>4 \mathrm{~V}</m> respectively.
				</p>
				<ol>
					<li>
						<p>
							What is the voltage difference across the terminals of the battery?
						</p>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>
							If the current through resistor 2 is <m>1.75 \mathrm{~A}</m>, what is the current through resistor 1?
						</p>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>
							Given the information from parts (a) and (b), what is the resistance of resistor 1?
						</p>
					</li>
				</ol>
			</statement>
			<answer>
				<p>
					<m>7 \mathrm{~V}</m>
				</p>
			</answer>
			<answer>
				<p>
					<m>1.75 \mathrm{~A}</m>
				</p>
			</answer>
			<answer>
				<p>
					<m>1.71 \mathrm{~\Omega}</m>
				</p>
			</answer>
		</exploration>

		<figure xml:id="bs-circuit-12">
			<title>Circuit Diagram</title>
			<caption>A circuit diagram.</caption>
			<image source="circ/circuit-12.png" width = "50%" >
			<description>A circuit diagram with a battery on the left, a downward branch with 25 Ohms, a rightward branch with 15 Ohms, another downward branch with 15 Ohms, and a final leftward branch with 20 Ohms.</description></image>
		</figure>

		<exploration>
			<title>Current Through Resistors</title>
			<statement><p></p>
				<p>
					Consider the circuit above.
				</p>
				<ol>
					<li>
						<p>
							Through which resistor does the most current flow?
						</p>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>
							Through which resistor does the least current flow?
						</p>
					</li>
				</ol>
			</statement>
			<answer>
				<p>
					The most current flows through the <m>20 \mathrm{~\Omega}</m> resistor.
				</p>
			</answer>
			<answer>
				<p>
					The least amount of current flows through each of the <m>15 \mathrm{~\Omega}</m> resistor.
				</p>
			</answer>
		</exploration>

		<exploration>
			<title>Increasing Resistance</title>
			<statement><p></p>
				<p>
					Two identical resistors are connected in parallel across the terminals of a battery.  If you increase the resistance of one of the resistors, what happens to the current through, and the voltage difference across, the other resistor? 
				</p>
			</statement>
			<answer>
				<p>
					The current and voltage difference remain the same, if the battery is ideal.
				</p>
			</answer>
		</exploration>

		<figure xml:id="bs-circuit-13">
			<title>Circuit Diagram</title>
			<caption>A circuit diagram.</caption>
			<image source="circ/circuit-13.png" width = "50%" >
			<description>A 12 V battery connected to a 15 Ohm resistor, a parallel network of two five Ohm resistors, and an eight Ohm resistor.</description></image>
		</figure>

		<exploration>
			<title>Circuit Analysis</title>
			<statement><p></p>
				<p>
					Consider the resistive circuit shown above.
				</p>
				<ol>
					<li>
						<p>
							Is the current going through <m>R_4</m> greater than, less than, or equal to the current going through <m>R_1</m>? 
						</p>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>
							Is the current going through <m>R_4</m> greater than, less than, or equal to the current going through <m>R_2</m>?
						</p>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>
							What is the decrease in voltage across <m>R_3</m>? 
						</p>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>
							How much current is passing through point P?
						</p>
					</li>
				</ol>
			</statement>
			<answer>
				<p>
					Equal to.
				</p>
			</answer>
			<answer>
				<p>
					Greater than.
				</p>
			</answer>
			<answer>
				<p>
					<m>1.18 \mathrm{~V}</m>
				</p>
			</answer>
			<answer>
				<p>
					<m>0.235 \mathrm{~A}</m>
				</p>
			</answer>
		</exploration>

		<figure xml:id="bs-circuit-14">
			<title>Circuit Diagram</title>
			<caption>A circuit diagram.</caption>
			<image source="circ/circuit-14.png" width = "50%" >
			<description>A circuit diagram with three vertical branches, from left to right: VA, R3, VB.  The upward horizontal wires contain, from left to right, R1 and R2.</description></image>
		</figure>

		<exploration>
			<title>Circuit Analysis II</title>
			<statement><p></p>
				<p>
					Consider the circuit shown above. 
				</p>
				<p>
					If <m>V_A = 6 \ \mathrm{V}</m>, <m>V_B = 2 \ \mathrm{V}</m>, <m>R_1 = 4 \ \Omega</m>, <m>R_2 = 1 \ \Omega</m>, and <m>R_3 = 3 \ \Omega</m>, find the magnitude of the current through resistor 1. 
				</p>
			</statement>
			<answer>
				<p>
					<m>0.947 \mathrm{~A}</m>
				</p>
			</answer>
		</exploration>

		<figure xml:id="bs-circuit-segment">
			<title>Circuit Segment</title>
			<caption>A circuit segment.</caption>
			<image source="circ/circuit-segment.png" width = "50%" >
			<description>A vertical portion of a circuit diagram: four parallel resistors of 4, 4, 2, and 4 Ohms, followed by another two parallel resistors of 4 and 2 Ohms.</description></image>
		</figure>

		<exploration>
			<title>Circuit Segment</title>
			<statement><p></p>
				<p>
					What is the equivalent resistance of the circuit segment above?
				</p>
			</statement>
			<answer>
				<p>
					<m>2 \mathrm{~\Omega}</m>
				</p>
			</answer>
		</exploration>

		<exploration>
			<title>Parallel Resistors</title>
			<statement><p></p>
				<p>
					Every time you add another resistor in parallel which of the following changes occur?
				</p>
				<ol>
					<li>
						<p>
							The voltage change across the battery increases.
						</p>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>
							The voltage change across the battery decreases.
						</p>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>
							The current coming through the battery increases.
						</p>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>
							The current coming through the battery decreases.
						</p>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>
							The total resistance of the circuit increases.
						</p>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>
							The total resistance of the circuit decreases.
						</p>
					</li>
				</ol>
			</statement>
			<answer>
				<p>
					(3), (6)
				</p>
			</answer>
		</exploration>

		<exploration>
			<title>Circuit Analysis II</title>
			<statement><p></p>
				<p>
					Recall the <xref ref="bs-circuit-13"/> from above. 
				</p>
				<ol>
					<li>
						<p>
							What is the equivalent resistance of the circuit?
						</p>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>
							How much current is passing through <m>R_1</m>? 
						</p>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>
							What is the voltage difference across <m>R_4</m>?
						</p>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>
							If another <m>5 \mathrm{~\Omega}</m> resistor were connected in parallel with <m>R_2</m> and <m>R_3</m>, would the overall resistance of the circuit increase, decrease, or stay the same?  
						</p>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>
							If another <m>5 \mathrm{~\Omega}</m> resistor were connected in parallel with <m>R_2</m> and <m>R_3</m>, would the voltage difference across the battery increase, decrease, or stay the same? 
						</p>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>
							If another <m>5 \mathrm{~\Omega}</m> resistor were connected in parallel with <m>R_2</m> and <m>R_3</m>, would the current drawn from the battery increase, decrease, or stay the same? 
						</p>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>
							If another <m>5 \mathrm{~\Omega}</m> resistor were connected in parallel with <m>R_2</m> and <m>R_3</m>, would the power dissipated by <m>R_4</m> increase, decrease, or stay the same? 
						</p>
					</li>
				</ol>
			</statement>
			<answer>
				<p>
					<m>25.5 \mathrm{~\Omega}</m>
				</p>
			</answer>
			<answer>
				<p>
					<m>0.471 \mathrm{~A}</m>
				</p>
			</answer>
			<answer>
				<p>
					<m>3.76 \mathrm{~V}</m>
				</p>
			</answer>
			<answer>
				<p>
					Decrease.
				</p>
			</answer>
			<answer>
				<p>
					Stay the same.
				</p>
			</answer>
			<answer>
				<p>
					Increase.
				</p>
			</answer>
			<answer>
				<p>
					Increase.
				</p>
			</answer>
		</exploration>

		<exploration>
			<title>Shock!</title>
			<statement><p></p>
				<p>
					The severity of a shock depends on the magnitude of the current through your body. Would you prefer to be in series or in parallel with a resistance, such as the heating element of a toaster, if you were shocked by it? Explain.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<answer>
				<p>
					It would probably be better to be in series because the current will be less than if it were in parallel.
				</p>
			</answer>
		</exploration>

		<exploration>
			<title>Smallest and Largest Resistances</title>
			<statement><p></p>
				<p>
					What are the largest and smallest resistances you can obtain by connecting a <m>36.0 \mathrm{~\Omega}</m>, a <m>50.0 \mathrm{~\Omega}</m>, and a <m>700 \mathrm{~\Omega}</m> resistor together?
				</p>
			</statement>
			<answer>
				<p>
					Largest: <m>786 \mathrm{~\Omega}</m>, Smallest: <m>20.32 \mathrm{~\Omega}</m>
				</p>
			</answer>
		</exploration>
	</subsubsection>
  
